# C++ Source Code Rules

## Modern C++ Standards
- Prefer C++17 or newer features
- Use smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) over raw pointers
- Use RAII for resource management
- Prefer `std::string_view` for read-only string parameters
- Use `std::optional` for optional values

## Memory Safety
- Avoid raw `new`/`delete` - use smart pointers or containers
- No manual memory management in application code
- Use `std::span` for array views (C++20)
- Prefer value semantics where reasonable

## Error Handling
- Use exceptions for exceptional conditions
- Use `std::expected` or `std::optional` for expected failures (C++23/17)
- Document exception specifications
- Use RAII to ensure cleanup on exceptions

## Naming Conventions
- Classes/Structs: `PascalCase`
- Functions/Methods: `snake_case` or `camelCase` (be consistent)
- Constants: `kConstantName` or `CONSTANT_NAME`
- Private members: `member_` or `m_member`

## Header Guidelines
- Use `#pragma once` or include guards
- Forward declare where possible
- Include what you use (IWYU)

## Testing
- Use GoogleTest, Catch2, or doctest
- Include requirement references in test comments
- Keep tests deterministic

```cpp
TEST(FeatureTest, HandlesValidInput) {
    // REQ-0001: System shall process valid input
    auto result = ProcessInput(valid_input);
    EXPECT_TRUE(result.is_ok());
}
```

## Build System
- Use CMake or similar modern build system
- Enable warnings: `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic`
- Consider: `-Werror` in CI

